WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.860
Welcome to Biomolecules.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.060
This recorded Chemistry class uses an AI generated Marin voice.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.480
Carbohydrates include sugars and their larger assemblies.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.880
Monosaccharides cannot be hydrolysed into simpler carbohydrate units.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:19.360
An aldose has an aldehyde group in its open chain representation; a ketose has

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:19.880
a ketone.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.080
Glucose is an aldohexose and fructose a ketohexose: both have six carbons, but

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.380
their carbonyl positions differ.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.620
Many sugars spend most of their time in cyclic forms in solution, so an open

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.500
chain formula is a useful representation rather than the only structure

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:36.940
present.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.520
Ring formation creates an anomeric centre.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.440
Do not identify a carbohydrate solely from the often used simple carbon and

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.040
water composition pattern; some compounds fit it without being carbohydrates

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.860
and some carbohydrate derivatives do not fit it.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.960
For glucose and fructose, six carbon atoms and the same overall composition do

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.820
not imply the same functional group position.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.340
Classification combines the carbon count with the open chain carbonyl type.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.900
Ring drawings add a further distinction between alpha and beta forms at an

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.160
anomeric centre.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.380
A formula comparison should therefore be followed by a connectivity check

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.420
before naming a sugar.

00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:16.088
Sucrose contains glucose and fructose units.

00:01:16.948 --> 00:01:21.828
Maltose contains two glucose units, while lactose contains galactose and

00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:22.288
glucose.

00:01:23.268 --> 00:01:27.248
Joining two monosaccharides with a glycosidic bond has the net composition

00:01:27.248 --> 00:01:32.308
change of losing one water molecule; hydrolysis adds water and separates the

00:01:32.308 --> 00:01:32.708
units.

00:01:33.768 --> 00:01:37.568
Structural bonding details distinguish the three despite related overall

00:01:37.568 --> 00:01:38.008
formulas.

00:01:38.868 --> 00:01:43.168
A reducing sugar can access a reactive carbonyl form under the relevant test

00:01:43.168 --> 00:01:43.648
conditions.

00:01:45.048 --> 00:01:49.088
Sucrose has both anomeric centres involved in its linkage and is nonreducing

00:01:49.088 --> 00:01:50.128
before hydrolysis.

00:01:51.248 --> 00:01:55.208
Maltose and lactose retain a suitable free anomeric centre and are reducing.

00:01:56.228 --> 00:02:00.448
Fructose can respond in alkaline reducing sugar tests through rearrangement

00:02:00.568 --> 00:02:01.608
even though it is a ketose.

00:02:02.308 --> 00:02:06.628
For one mole of sucrose hydrolysed completely, one mole each of glucose and

00:02:06.628 --> 00:02:07.428
fructose appears.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:11.048
One mole of maltose gives two moles of glucose instead.

00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:15.588
The total monosaccharide amount doubles, while the carbon inventory is

00:02:15.588 --> 00:02:15.888
conserved.

00:02:16.908 --> 00:02:20.828
Water participates in hydrolysis, so adding the masses of the isolated sugar

00:02:20.828 --> 00:02:24.248
units need not equal the original dry disaccharide mass alone.

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:30.256
Starch and glycogen store glucose units, whereas cellulose has a structural

00:02:30.256 --> 00:02:30.576
role.

00:02:31.436 --> 00:02:34.856
Different linkage arrangements change properties even when the repeating

00:02:34.856 --> 00:02:36.336
monosaccharide is glucose.

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:41.856
This is an example of why a monomer name alone cannot specify the behaviour of

00:02:41.856 --> 00:02:42.596
a polymer.

00:02:43.036 --> 00:02:47.036
The official Chemistry unit emphasizes the named monosaccharides and the

00:02:47.036 --> 00:02:50.176
constituent units of sucrose, maltose and lactose.

00:02:50.896 --> 00:02:55.396
Use larger polysaccharides as supporting context rather than claiming every

00:02:55.396 --> 00:02:57.396
biological polymer detail is required.

00:02:58.296 --> 00:03:02.576
For a hydrolysis question, track the type and number of released units, not

00:03:02.576 --> 00:03:05.056
simply whether the original substance has a sweet taste.

00:03:06.056 --> 00:03:10.656
Amylose is largely unbranched, while amylopectin and glycogen have branching.

00:03:11.436 --> 00:03:15.316
Cellulose uses a different glucose linkage arrangement that gives extended

00:03:15.316 --> 00:03:16.236
structural chains.

00:03:17.016 --> 00:03:20.776
Humans do not digest cellulose with the same enzymes used for starch.

00:03:21.556 --> 00:03:25.936
These contrasts connect molecular bonding with macroscopic properties without

00:03:25.936 --> 00:03:28.256
changing the identity of the glucose building block.

00:03:29.176 --> 00:03:32.696
Linkage and branching must accompany the monomer name in a comparison.

00:03:33.336 --> 00:03:38.536
A typical alpha amino acid has amino and carboxyl groups associated with the

00:03:38.536 --> 00:03:39.456
same alpha carbon.

00:03:40.196 --> 00:03:42.836
Its side chain distinguishes it from other amino acids.

00:03:43.676 --> 00:03:48.076
In suitable aqueous conditions, it can exist as a zwitterion carrying both

00:03:48.076 --> 00:03:53.256
positive and negative charges; the dominant ionic form depends on p H and the

00:03:53.256 --> 00:03:53.656
side chain.

00:03:54.636 --> 00:03:58.816
A peptide bond is the amide linkage with a carbonyl carbon bonded to nitrogen

00:03:59.376 --> 00:04:01.036
formed between amino acid residues.

00:04:01.916 --> 00:04:06.976
The net assembly of a simple linear chain of n amino acids forms n minus one

00:04:06.976 --> 00:04:11.616
peptide links and releases n minus one waters in a condensation bookkeeping

00:04:11.616 --> 00:04:11.936
model.

00:04:13.356 --> 00:04:16.896
Living synthesis is more elaborate than directly mixing free amino acids.

00:04:17.696 --> 00:04:19.836
Hydrolysis breaks the links and adds water.

00:04:20.856 --> 00:04:25.077
For five amino acid residues in one noncyclic linear chain, four joins are

00:04:25.077 --> 00:04:25.476
needed.

00:04:25.476 --> 00:04:29.876
That gives four peptide bonds and four net water losses in the simple

00:04:29.876 --> 00:04:30.596
bookkeeping model.

00:04:31.636 --> 00:04:35.576
A cyclic peptide or several separate chains would require different counting.

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:40.666
Glycine has two hydrogens at its alpha carbon, unlike a typical amino acid

00:04:40.666 --> 00:04:40.876
with

00:04:40.876 --> 00:04:45.636
four distinct attached groups, so do not automatically assign every amino acid

00:04:45.636 --> 00:04:47.236
a stereogenic alpha carbon.

00:04:47.496 --> 00:04:50.296
Protein structure is described at four different levels.

00:04:51.256 --> 00:04:54.596
Primary structure gives the amino acid sequence along a polypeptide chain.

00:04:55.456 --> 00:04:59.076
Secondary structure describes recurring local backbone arrangements, including

00:04:59.076 --> 00:05:02.816
alpha helices and beta sheets, with hydrogen bonds playing an important

00:05:02.816 --> 00:05:03.696
stabilizing role.

00:05:04.716 --> 00:05:07.776
Tertiary structure is the complete three dimensional folding of one chain.

00:05:08.696 --> 00:05:12.176
Quaternary structure describes how several polypeptide subunits are arranged

00:05:12.176 --> 00:05:12.516
together.

00:05:13.096 --> 00:05:16.736
A single chain protein can have the first three levels without having

00:05:16.736 --> 00:05:17.576
quaternary structure.

00:05:18.416 --> 00:05:20.736
Keep the sequence separate from the way that sequence folds.

00:05:21.756 --> 00:05:25.416
Denaturation changes the folded organization and can reduce biological

00:05:25.416 --> 00:05:25.856
activity.

00:05:26.636 --> 00:05:30.374
It does not necessarily break peptide bonds or turn the whole protein into

00:05:30.374 --> 00:05:30.576
free

00:05:30.576 --> 00:05:31.256
amino acids.

00:05:32.116 --> 00:05:35.436
Heat and changes in chemical environment can disturb stabilizing interactions,

00:05:35.996 --> 00:05:37.716
but different proteins respond differently.

00:05:38.616 --> 00:05:42.116
Refolding is not guaranteed simply by restoring the original temperature.

00:05:42.416 --> 00:05:46.356
In a comparison question, identify whether the evidence concerns sequence,

00:05:46.996 --> 00:05:50.936
local backbone folding, the shape of one complete chain or the association of

00:05:50.936 --> 00:05:51.496
several chains.

00:05:52.396 --> 00:05:56.276
Loss of activity with an intact peptide backbone supports a folding change

00:05:56.276 --> 00:05:58.076
rather than complete hydrolysis.

00:05:58.464 --> 00:06:04.304
Enzymes are biological catalysts; most are proteins, though some catalytic RNA

00:06:04.304 --> 00:06:05.624
molecules also exist.

00:06:06.464 --> 00:06:11.244
A suitable active site helps bind substrates and provide a lower barrier

00:06:11.244 --> 00:06:11.764
pathway.

00:06:13.084 --> 00:06:17.924
Specificity reflects structure and interactions, not an absolute promise that

00:06:17.924 --> 00:06:20.844
one enzyme acts on only one possible molecule.

00:06:21.744 --> 00:06:26.764
Like other catalysts, enzymes do not change the equilibrium constant for a

00:06:26.764 --> 00:06:27.764
reaction at the same conditions.

00:06:29.384 --> 00:06:33.220
Activity depends on factors such as temperature and p H, and extreme

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:33.784
conditions

00:06:33.784 --> 00:06:34.864
can damage structure.

00:06:35.944 --> 00:06:39.664
More heat does not mean unlimited acceleration because the catalyst can lose

00:06:39.664 --> 00:06:40.464
its working form.

00:06:41.044 --> 00:06:45.244
An enzyme changes the barrier along a reaction pathway and can accelerate both

00:06:45.244 --> 00:06:46.524
directions toward equilibrium.

00:06:47.144 --> 00:06:51.639
It does not force a different final equilibrium composition merely by being a

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:52.164
catalyst.

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:56.704
Changing acidity or temperature can change both the chemical environment and

00:06:56.704 --> 00:06:57.604
the enzyme structure.

00:06:58.904 --> 00:07:02.624
Compare activity measurements only when the substrate amount, conditions and

00:07:02.624 --> 00:07:04.564
measured interval are appropriately controlled.

00:07:04.872 --> 00:07:10.272
Vitamins are needed in small amounts for normal biochemical functions.

00:07:11.292 --> 00:07:16.992
The usual classification places A, D, E and K among fat soluble vitamins

00:07:17.392 --> 00:07:20.612
and the B group and C among water soluble vitamins.

00:07:21.652 --> 00:07:25.612
These are families with distinct roles, not one interchangeable chemical.

00:07:26.672 --> 00:07:31.412
Hormones are chemical signals acting through suitable biological targets; some

00:07:31.412 --> 00:07:35.392
are peptides, some steroids and others have different structures.

00:07:36.372 --> 00:07:41.267
Insulin is a peptide hormone example, whereas many steroid hormones derive

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:41.532
from

00:07:41.532 --> 00:07:42.892
a different molecular framework.

00:07:44.232 --> 00:07:48.032
Vitamin classification and hormone classification answer different questions.

00:07:49.132 --> 00:07:53.352
Solubility places vitamins into broad groups; it does not state a universal

00:07:53.352 --> 00:07:55.712
molecular structure or biochemical role.

00:07:56.552 --> 00:08:01.472
A peptide hormone has amino acid residues joined in a chain, while a steroid

00:08:01.472 --> 00:08:03.312
hormone has a different carbon framework.

00:08:04.352 --> 00:08:08.512
Recognize insulin as a peptide example and keep the two classification systems

00:08:08.512 --> 00:08:09.572
separate during revision.

00:08:10.852 --> 00:08:14.232
Recognizing a family name does not uniquely determine every reaction or

00:08:14.232 --> 00:08:14.872
functional group.

00:08:15.972 --> 00:08:19.995
Compare the actual molecular framework when a structure is supplied rather

00:08:19.995 --> 00:08:20.212
than

00:08:20.212 --> 00:08:23.552
treating all biological signalling compounds as the same class.

00:08:23.832 --> 00:08:27.032
A nucleoside combines a nitrogenous base and a sugar.

00:08:27.852 --> 00:08:31.572
Adding a phosphate component gives a nucleotide, the building block of nucleic

00:08:31.572 --> 00:08:31.932
acids.

00:08:32.892 --> 00:08:38.652
DNA uses deoxyribose and typically the bases A, G, C and T; RNA uses

00:08:38.652 --> 00:08:41.192
ribose and usually A, G, C and U.

00:08:41.772 --> 00:08:45.172
The sugar phosphate backbone joins units through phosphodiester links.

00:08:45.852 --> 00:08:50.852
In the elementary double stranded DNA model, A pairs with T and G with C, with

00:08:50.852 --> 00:08:52.492
complementary antiparallel strands.

00:08:53.052 --> 00:08:57.612
DNA stores hereditary information, while RNA has roles in expressing and

00:08:57.612 --> 00:08:58.172
processing it.

00:08:59.012 --> 00:09:03.352
Complementary DNA base count rules apply to a whole double stranded sample; do

00:09:03.352 --> 00:09:07.132
not impose equal A and T on an arbitrary single RNA strand.

00:09:07.892 --> 00:09:11.123
If adenine accounts for thirty percent of a whole complementary double

00:09:11.123 --> 00:09:11.492
stranded

00:09:11.492 --> 00:09:14.679
DNA sample, thymine is also thirty percent.

00:09:14.679 --> 00:09:18.752
Forty percent remains for guanine plus cytosine, giving twenty percent each.

00:09:19.182 --> 00:09:22.332
This calculation uses complementarity over both strands.

00:09:23.112 --> 00:09:27.932
A single isolated strand can have unequal adenine and thymine counts, and RNA

00:09:27.932 --> 00:09:31.732
substitutes uracil for thymine in the usual elementary comparison.

00:09:32.232 --> 00:09:36.552
A Fischer projection encodes stereochemistry and is not simply a vertical

00:09:36.552 --> 00:09:37.492
decorative formula.

00:09:38.332 --> 00:09:42.372
For open chain D glucose, place the aldehyde group at the top and the terminal

00:09:42.372 --> 00:09:43.812
hydroxymethyl group at the bottom.

00:09:44.532 --> 00:09:48.732
The hydroxyl positions from carbon two through carbon five are right, left,

00:09:49.092 --> 00:09:49.952
right and right.

00:09:50.992 --> 00:09:54.652
Horizontal bonds point toward the viewer and vertical bonds point away in this

00:09:54.652 --> 00:09:55.532
projection convention.

00:09:56.492 --> 00:10:00.332
Carbon one belongs to the aldehyde; carbon six belongs to the terminal

00:10:00.332 --> 00:10:01.332
hydroxymethyl group.

00:10:01.952 --> 00:10:05.252
The D label is assigned by the configuration at the highest numbered

00:10:05.252 --> 00:10:09.032
stereogenic centre in this standard comparison, which is carbon five here.

00:10:09.812 --> 00:10:11.932
It does not mean every hydroxyl lies on the right.

00:10:12.512 --> 00:10:15.372
Nor does D automatically predict positive optical rotation.

00:10:16.412 --> 00:10:20.292
Changing one stereogenic centre can give a different sugar; merely rotating a

00:10:20.292 --> 00:10:21.692
whole spatial molecule does not.

00:10:22.512 --> 00:10:26.412
The projection is a useful open chain representation even though aqueous

00:10:26.412 --> 00:10:27.792
glucose is mainly cyclic.

00:10:28.442 --> 00:10:32.412
Keep carbon numbering visible when connecting the chain to a ring so that the

00:10:32.412 --> 00:10:34.192
anomeric carbon can be identified correctly.

00:10:34.488 --> 00:10:39.508
When glucose forms a six membered pyranose ring, the carbon five hydroxyl

00:10:39.508 --> 00:10:43.368
oxygen connects with the carbon one aldehyde carbon to make a cyclic

00:10:43.368 --> 00:10:44.088
hemiacetal.

00:10:45.048 --> 00:10:47.788
Carbon one becomes the anomeric centre.

00:10:47.788 --> 00:10:51.268
Two configurations are possible there while the other stereogenic centres

00:10:51.268 --> 00:10:52.428
retain their relationships.

00:10:53.128 --> 00:10:57.168
In the standard Haworth representation of D glucopyranose with the

00:10:57.168 --> 00:11:02.328
hydroxymethyl group drawn up, an anomeric hydroxyl drawn down is alpha and one

00:11:02.328 --> 00:11:03.408
drawn up is beta.

00:11:03.988 --> 00:11:08.048
More generally, compare the anomeric hydroxyl with the reference hydroxymethyl

00:11:08.048 --> 00:11:11.888
group: trans gives alpha and cis gives beta for this representation.

00:11:12.488 --> 00:11:16.148
The schematic highlights those groups and does not show every hydroxyl.

00:11:16.868 --> 00:11:20.488
The two anomers can interconvert through ring opening and closure in solution,

00:11:21.208 --> 00:11:22.148
producing mutarotation.

00:11:22.588 --> 00:11:26.408
They are not different molecular formulas and they are not simply two

00:11:26.408 --> 00:11:29.168
conformations of one unchanged anomeric configuration.

00:11:29.948 --> 00:11:33.368
Alpha and beta labels also do not mean the signs of optical rotation.

00:11:34.208 --> 00:11:37.946
A chair conformation supplies further three dimensional information beyond

00:11:37.946 --> 00:11:38.148
this

00:11:38.148 --> 00:11:42.168
simplified ring projection, which should not be treated as an exact planar

00:11:42.168 --> 00:11:42.888
molecular shape.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.680
A free anomeric hemiacetal can open to a carbonyl form and provides a useful

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.440
way to identify a reducing end.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:55.320
Maltose and lactose retain a free anomeric centre and are reducing

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:57.300
disaccharides in the usual comparison.

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:03.040
Sucrose links the anomeric centres of its glucose and fructose units, so it

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:05.880
lacks that free reducing end before hydrolysis.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:11.436
Its hydrolysis gives one mole of glucose and one mole of fructose per mole of

00:12:11.436 --> 00:12:11.940
sucrose.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:16.925
With rounded molar masses, three hundred forty two grams of sucrose reacts

00:12:16.925 --> 00:12:17.180
with

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:20.320
eighteen grams of water to give one hundred eighty grams of each

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:20.980
monosaccharide.

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:26.140
The total product mass is three hundred sixty grams; water has become part of

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:28.720
the products rather than mass appearing from nowhere.

00:12:29.660 --> 00:12:33.500
Fructose can give reducing test behaviour under suitable alkaline conditions

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:39.060
despite its open chain ketone classification, so aldehyde naming alone is not

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:40.020
the complete criterion.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:44.420
Distinguish a sugar before hydrolysis from the mixture produced afterward.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:49.260
A change in a test result can reveal a changed linkage or newly accessible

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:49.960
reducing end.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:54.849
An amino acid contains groups that can gain or lose protons, so its

00:12:54.849 --> 00:12:55.580
predominant

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:57.820
charge depends on p H.

00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:01.140
Glycine provides a simple example without an ionizable side chain.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:06.288
In sufficiently acidic solution, its amino group is protonated and its

00:13:06.288 --> 00:13:06.780
carboxyl

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:10.080
group is mostly neutral, giving net charge plus one.

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:15.180
In the intermediate zwitterionic form, the amino group remains positive while

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:18.160
the carboxylate is negative, giving net charge zero.

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:23.700
In sufficiently basic solution, the carboxylate remains negative and the amino

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.560
group is neutral, giving net charge minus one.

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:31.480
A zwitterion is therefore not a structure with no formal charges; it has

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:33.380
opposite charges in the same molecule.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:38.400
At the isoelectric point, the average net charge is zero under the stated

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:38.880
conditions.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:43.300
This does not mean every molecule has permanently lost both acid base

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:43.700
functions.

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:49.360
Amino acids with ionizable side chains need additional protonation states and

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:52.280
cannot be analysed by copying glycine's pattern alone.

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:57.340
Identify all ionizable groups, compare the p H with the relevant dissociation

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.320
constants, and then add their charges.

00:13:59.808 --> 00:14:05.668
A peptide bond joins a carboxyl carbon of one amino acid to the nitrogen of

00:14:05.668 --> 00:14:09.548
another, with loss of water in the simplified condensation account.

00:14:10.008 --> 00:14:15.188
A linear chain with five residues has four peptide links and loses four water

00:14:15.188 --> 00:14:18.088
molecules when assembled from those free amino acids.

00:14:18.948 --> 00:14:23.500
A cyclic chain has different end group bookkeeping, so the linear rule must be

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:23.908
stated.

00:14:24.748 --> 00:14:28.928
Sequence is directional: glycine followed by alanine is not the same

00:14:28.928 --> 00:14:33.908
connectivity as alanine followed by glycine, although the two dipeptides have

00:14:33.908 --> 00:14:35.288
the same total composition.

00:14:35.288 --> 00:14:40.368
Write from the amino terminus to the carboxyl terminus in the usual sequence

00:14:40.368 --> 00:14:40.848
convention.

00:14:41.668 --> 00:14:46.298
Using illustrative rounded molar masses of seventy five for glycine and eighty

00:14:46.298 --> 00:14:51.368
nine for alanine, forming one dipeptide gives seventy five plus eighty nine

00:14:51.368 --> 00:14:54.728
minus eighteen, or one hundred forty six grams per mole.

00:14:55.708 --> 00:14:57.708
Hydrolysis reverses the water balance.

00:14:58.288 --> 00:15:02.648
This calculation concerns one condensation link, not the loss of an entire

00:15:02.648 --> 00:15:05.368
hydroxyl or amino group from the residue count.

00:15:06.548 --> 00:15:10.428
Distinguish sequence, number of residues, number of links and mass balance

00:15:10.428 --> 00:15:12.448
before comparing peptide formulas.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:17.600
A DNA base percentage problem must say whether its data describe one strand or

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:19.020
the complete paired molecule.

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:24.300
For ordinary double stranded DNA with canonical complementary pairing, adenine

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.680
pairs with thymine and guanine with cytosine.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:31.580
Across the complete two strand molecule, adenine and thymine counts are equal,

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:33.960
and guanine and cytosine counts are equal.

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:38.760
If adenine is thirty percent of all bases, thymine is also thirty percent,

00:15:38.760 --> 00:15:42.140
leaving forty percent shared equally between guanine and cytosine.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:44.700
Each of those is twenty percent.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.520
Take a molecule containing one thousand base pairs, hence two thousand

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:49.340
individual bases.

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:53.820
The counts are six hundred adenines, six hundred thymines, four hundred

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:55.740
guanines and four hundred cytosines.

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:59.640
There are six hundred A T pairs and four hundred G C pairs.

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:04.680
Counting two hydrogen bonds per A T pair and three per G C pair gives two

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:07.060
thousand four hundred in the standard pairing model.

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:11.580
One isolated strand need not have equal adenine and thymine percentages.

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:16.980
Mismatches, single stranded material or noncanonical chemistry require the

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.640
stated model to be reconsidered.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:22.980
Hydrogen bond counts alone also do not determine a DNA melting temperature,

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:25.960
which depends on more structural and solution factors.

00:16:26.232 --> 00:16:31.252
The terms base, nucleoside and nucleotide identify different amounts of

00:16:31.252 --> 00:16:31.732
molecular structure.

00:16:32.772 --> 00:16:35.952
A nitrogenous base attached to a pentose sugar is a nucleoside.

00:16:36.572 --> 00:16:38.572
Including phosphate gives a nucleotide.

00:16:39.152 --> 00:16:43.472
In the familiar nucleic acid backbone, phosphodiester links connect the three

00:16:43.472 --> 00:16:46.932
prime position of one sugar with the five prime position associated with the

00:16:46.932 --> 00:16:47.812
next nucleotide.

00:16:48.652 --> 00:16:51.952
The primes distinguish sugar carbon numbering from the base numbering.

00:16:51.952 --> 00:16:57.772
DNA normally contains deoxyribose, whereas RNA contains ribose with a hydroxyl

00:16:57.772 --> 00:16:58.952
at the two prime position.

00:17:00.452 --> 00:17:03.892
That sugar difference is separate from the usual thymine versus uracil base

00:17:03.892 --> 00:17:04.372
comparison.

00:17:05.532 --> 00:17:09.532
For an unbranched linear strand containing ten nucleotide residues, there are

00:17:09.532 --> 00:17:11.892
nine internucleotide phosphodiester links.

00:17:12.532 --> 00:17:16.612
This does not mean there are always exactly nine phosphate groups: a terminal

00:17:16.612 --> 00:17:19.852
phosphate may add another, depending on how the ends are specified.

00:17:20.372 --> 00:17:24.472
A circular unbranched strand has a different link count because its ends join.

00:17:25.532 --> 00:17:29.592
Likewise, a double stranded DNA fragment consists of two covalently separate

00:17:29.592 --> 00:17:33.852
backbones connected through base pairing, not one continuous strand crossing

00:17:33.852 --> 00:17:34.732
between the bases.

00:17:35.652 --> 00:17:39.352
Count residues, links and terminal groups as distinct quantities before

00:17:39.352 --> 00:17:40.212
inferring composition.

00:17:40.488 --> 00:17:43.968
Protein structure can change without cutting its amino acid sequence into

00:17:43.968 --> 00:17:44.808
separate molecules.

00:17:45.808 --> 00:17:50.426
Ordinary denaturation disrupts the interactions that maintain a native fold,

00:17:50.426 --> 00:17:50.548
so

00:17:50.548 --> 00:17:55.313
secondary, tertiary or quaternary organization and biological activity can be

00:17:55.313 --> 00:17:55.808
altered.

00:17:56.448 --> 00:18:00.508
The peptide backbone is not normally hydrolyzed merely by calling a process

00:18:00.508 --> 00:18:01.348
denaturation.

00:18:02.188 --> 00:18:06.246
Hydrolysis instead cleaves peptide bonds with chemical consumption of water

00:18:06.246 --> 00:18:06.408
and

00:18:06.408 --> 00:18:07.788
creates additional chain ends.

00:18:08.168 --> 00:18:13.008
For one ideal linear pentapeptide with no crosslinks, there are four peptide

00:18:13.008 --> 00:18:13.308
bonds.

00:18:14.388 --> 00:18:18.788
Complete hydrolysis consumes four water molecules per peptide molecule and

00:18:18.788 --> 00:18:20.648
gives five amino acid molecules.

00:18:21.808 --> 00:18:25.648
Breaking only one internal peptide bond gives two shorter peptide fragments,

00:18:26.208 --> 00:18:27.588
not five free amino acids.

00:18:28.708 --> 00:18:32.448
Disulfide bridges are another covalent connection: changing their oxidation

00:18:32.448 --> 00:18:35.848
state is distinct from cleaving an amide bond in the main chain.

00:18:36.568 --> 00:18:40.928
Real unfolding can be accompanied by aggregation, side reactions or loss of

00:18:40.928 --> 00:18:44.588
disulfide structure under particular treatments, so reversibility is not

00:18:44.588 --> 00:18:44.968
guaranteed.

00:18:45.808 --> 00:18:49.394
The useful distinction is which bonds and interactions are specified to

00:18:49.394 --> 00:18:49.748
change.

00:18:50.588 --> 00:18:55.068
Do not infer complete amino acid release or a new primary sequence solely from

00:18:55.068 --> 00:18:57.148
loss of native shape or enzyme activity.

00:18:57.456 --> 00:19:02.341
Mutarotation describes a change in optical rotation as an interconverting

00:19:02.341 --> 00:19:02.676
sugar

00:19:02.676 --> 00:19:04.976
mixture approaches its equilibrium composition.

00:19:05.816 --> 00:19:10.216
For a numerical glucose example, suppose the supplied specific rotations are

00:19:10.216 --> 00:19:14.816
plus one hundred twelve for the alpha form, plus eighteen point seven for the

00:19:14.816 --> 00:19:20.036
beta form, and plus fifty two point seven for the equilibrium mixture, all at

00:19:20.036 --> 00:19:20.956
matching measurement conditions.

00:19:21.796 --> 00:19:25.996
Use an explicit two form model that neglects minor open chain and other ring

00:19:25.996 --> 00:19:26.276
forms.

00:19:26.776 --> 00:19:28.896
Let the alpha fraction be f.

00:19:29.716 --> 00:19:34.176
The weighted rotation is one hundred twelve times f plus eighteen point seven

00:19:34.176 --> 00:19:35.636
times one minus f.

00:19:36.556 --> 00:19:40.436
Solving gives f equal to thirty four divided by ninety three point three,

00:19:41.236 --> 00:19:43.776
approximately zero point three six four.

00:19:43.776 --> 00:19:47.956
The beta fraction is therefore about zero point six three six.

00:19:47.956 --> 00:19:52.339
Both pure forms are dextrorotatory in this example, so the equilibrium is not

00:19:52.339 --> 00:19:52.396
a

00:19:52.396 --> 00:19:54.076
racemate with zero rotation.

00:19:55.196 --> 00:19:58.796
Alpha and beta describe the anomeric relationship, while D describes

00:19:58.796 --> 00:20:02.736
configuration relative to a reference structure; these labels do not by

00:20:02.736 --> 00:20:05.456
themselves state the sign of optical rotation for every sugar.

00:20:06.176 --> 00:20:10.496
The estimate uses the supplied rotations and additive mixture model, rather

00:20:10.496 --> 00:20:14.036
than claiming that every glucose solution at every temperature has exactly

00:20:14.036 --> 00:20:14.676
these fractions.

00:20:14.904 --> 00:20:20.604
Remembering that vitamins A D E and K are fat soluble is only the

00:20:20.604 --> 00:20:21.964
first classification step.

00:20:22.524 --> 00:20:24.484
Their physiological roles are distinct.

00:20:25.444 --> 00:20:29.024
Vitamin A contributes to the visual system, including the light sensitive

00:20:29.024 --> 00:20:32.724
retinal pigment system, and deficiency can cause night blindness.

00:20:33.844 --> 00:20:37.544
Vitamin D supports calcium and phosphate handling and normal bone

00:20:37.544 --> 00:20:40.824
mineralization; deficiency in children can cause rickets.

00:20:41.684 --> 00:20:46.104
Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant that helps protect cell membranes against

00:20:46.104 --> 00:20:47.144
oxidative damage.

00:20:48.264 --> 00:20:51.984
Vitamin K supports the activation of proteins involved in blood clotting,

00:20:52.684 --> 00:20:55.564
rather than serving as the physical material from which a clot is built.

00:20:56.644 --> 00:21:00.484
These are useful textbook associations, not a claim that every symptom

00:21:00.484 --> 00:21:04.044
identifies one vitamin or that each vitamin has only one function.

00:21:05.164 --> 00:21:09.204
None should be treated as a carbohydrate fuel simply because vitamins support

00:21:09.204 --> 00:21:10.084
normal metabolism.

00:21:11.544 --> 00:21:15.684
Solubility class also does not make the four molecules chemically identical or

00:21:15.684 --> 00:21:16.364
interchangeable.

00:21:17.064 --> 00:21:21.564
In a matching question, first read whether the prompt asks about solubility, a

00:21:21.564 --> 00:21:24.624
physiological role or a familiar deficiency association.

00:21:25.804 --> 00:21:29.524
Keeping those categories separate prevents a correct class label from becoming

00:21:29.524 --> 00:21:31.004
an incorrect functional answer.

00:21:31.224 --> 00:21:36.204
Water soluble vitamins include the B group and vitamin C, but this category

00:21:36.204 --> 00:21:38.564
contains many different molecular functions.

00:21:39.384 --> 00:21:43.984
Thiamine, vitamin B one, contributes through its active cofactor form to

00:21:43.984 --> 00:21:47.644
important metabolic reactions, including carbohydrate metabolism.

00:21:48.564 --> 00:21:51.044
Beriberi is a familiar deficiency association.

00:21:51.964 --> 00:21:57.184
Vitamin B twelve supports normal red blood cell formation, DNA synthesis and

00:21:57.184 --> 00:21:58.064
nervous system function.

00:21:58.804 --> 00:22:02.681
Although it is water soluble, substantial body stores, especially in the

00:22:02.681 --> 00:22:03.004
liver,

00:22:03.444 --> 00:22:04.464
can last for years.

00:22:05.184 --> 00:22:09.224
Therefore the statement that every water soluble vitamin is immediately lost

00:22:09.904 --> 00:22:11.164
without storage is false.

00:22:12.104 --> 00:22:16.324
Vitamin C, also called ascorbic acid, is required for normal collagen

00:22:16.324 --> 00:22:19.244
synthesis; severe deficiency causes scurvy.

00:22:20.064 --> 00:22:24.224
Collagen is a structural protein, while vitamin C supports the biochemical

00:22:24.224 --> 00:22:26.064
processes needed to make it properly.

00:22:26.264 --> 00:22:30.644
The vitamin does not become a peptide residue merely because it helps protein

00:22:30.644 --> 00:22:31.104
formation.

00:22:31.884 --> 00:22:36.404
These examples are educational function and deficiency links, not diagnostic

00:22:36.404 --> 00:22:37.464
rules for an individual.

00:22:38.184 --> 00:22:41.944
In exam matching, use the named vitamin and the particular role together

00:22:42.584 --> 00:22:45.784
instead of treating every B vitamin as a substitute for every other one.

00:22:45.984 --> 00:22:50.504
Compare the open chain Fischer projections of glucose and fructose without

00:22:50.504 --> 00:22:52.824
confusing carbonyl position with configuration.

00:22:54.004 --> 00:22:58.384
D fructose has six carbons, with terminal hydroxymethyl groups at carbon one

00:22:58.384 --> 00:22:59.344
and carbon six.

00:22:59.964 --> 00:23:02.164
Its carbonyl is a ketone at carbon two.

00:23:02.744 --> 00:23:06.924
In the standard projection with carbon one at the top, the hydroxyl groups at

00:23:06.924 --> 00:23:10.464
carbons three, four and five lie left, right and right, respectively.

00:23:11.164 --> 00:23:15.364
These three carbons are stereogenic in the open chain structure, whereas the

00:23:15.364 --> 00:23:18.804
carbonyl carbon is not a tetrahedral stereocentre.

00:23:18.804 --> 00:23:22.964
For the fixed unbranched two ketohexose connectivity, three independent

00:23:22.964 --> 00:23:24.964
stereocentres allow eight configurations.

00:23:25.844 --> 00:23:27.044
D fructose is one of them.

00:23:27.844 --> 00:23:31.664
The right hand hydroxyl at the highest numbered stereocentre, carbon five,

00:23:32.344 --> 00:23:36.258
provides the D reference here; D does not by itself predict the sign of

00:23:36.258 --> 00:23:36.644
optical

00:23:36.644 --> 00:23:37.164
rotation.

00:23:38.124 --> 00:23:41.584
Fischer horizontal bonds point toward the viewer and vertical bonds away.

00:23:42.044 --> 00:23:46.664
In solution, fructose also forms cyclic structures, where the former carbonyl

00:23:46.664 --> 00:23:49.304
carbon two becomes the anomeric centre.

00:23:49.304 --> 00:23:53.004
This open chain drawing is therefore a configuration reference, not a claim

00:23:53.004 --> 00:23:55.264
that every dissolved molecule remains in that form.

00:23:55.752 --> 00:23:59.732
A DNA complement question is incomplete until the strand directions are

00:23:59.732 --> 00:24:00.292
specified.

00:24:01.092 --> 00:24:05.172
Take the sequence adenine guanine thymine cytosine written from five prime to

00:24:05.172 --> 00:24:06.352
three prime.

00:24:06.352 --> 00:24:10.832
Directly beneath it, base pairing gives thymine cytosine adenine guanine, but

00:24:10.832 --> 00:24:14.852
that aligned complementary strand runs from three prime to five prime.

00:24:14.852 --> 00:24:18.992
If the answer must also be written from five prime to three prime, reverse the

00:24:18.992 --> 00:24:20.352
order of that complementary sequence.

00:24:20.812 --> 00:24:23.412
The answer is guanine adenine cytosine thymine.

00:24:24.252 --> 00:24:27.832
Merely replacing each letter without reversing gives an aligned complement in

00:24:27.832 --> 00:24:30.912
the opposite direction, not the requested five prime sequence.

00:24:31.532 --> 00:24:34.872
The short double stranded segment contains four base pairs and eight

00:24:34.872 --> 00:24:35.852
nucleotides in total.

00:24:36.572 --> 00:24:40.592
Two pairs are adenine thymine and two are guanine cytosine, giving ten

00:24:40.592 --> 00:24:43.052
interbase hydrogen bonds in the standard counting model.

00:24:43.792 --> 00:24:47.952
Each linear four nucleotide strand has three internucleotide phosphodiester

00:24:47.952 --> 00:24:51.372
connections, so the two backbones contain six such connections.

00:24:52.152 --> 00:24:55.484
That link count does not specify the exact number of terminal phosphate

00:24:55.484 --> 00:24:55.812
groups,

00:24:56.312 --> 00:24:58.112
because the end chemistry has not been given.

00:24:58.992 --> 00:25:02.552
Sequence direction, base pairing and backbone counting answer different parts

00:25:02.552 --> 00:25:03.892
of the same structural problem.

00:25:04.272 --> 00:25:08.032
Different reactions of glucose provide different structural evidence.

00:25:08.852 --> 00:25:13.390
In the familiar mild bromine water oxidation model, the accessible aldehyde

00:25:13.390 --> 00:25:13.572
end

00:25:13.572 --> 00:25:18.113
becomes a carboxyl group, giving the six carbon monocarboxylic product

00:25:18.113 --> 00:25:18.632
gluconic

00:25:18.632 --> 00:25:18.932
acid.

00:25:19.612 --> 00:25:25.016
In the stronger nitric acid oxidation comparison, both the aldehyde end and

00:25:25.016 --> 00:25:25.232
the

00:25:25.232 --> 00:25:29.856
terminal primary alcohol are oxidized, giving a six carbon dicarboxylic

00:25:29.856 --> 00:25:30.312
product

00:25:30.312 --> 00:25:32.532
called glucaric or saccharic acid.

00:25:33.212 --> 00:25:35.512
No carbon has been added in either change.

00:25:36.552 --> 00:25:40.432
Hydroxylamine reaction provides separate carbonyl evidence through oxime

00:25:40.432 --> 00:25:45.172
formation; it does not establish that every molecule remains in the open chain

00:25:45.172 --> 00:25:46.072
form in water.

00:25:47.132 --> 00:25:51.772
Acetylation to a pentaacetate supports the presence of five hydroxyl groups in

00:25:51.772 --> 00:25:56.172
glucose, while the cyclic equilibrium explains why a single static open chain

00:25:56.172 --> 00:25:57.552
drawing is incomplete.

00:25:59.172 --> 00:26:03.052
These observations answer different questions and should be combined rather

00:26:03.052 --> 00:26:05.012
than treated as interchangeable test names.

00:26:06.512 --> 00:26:11.612
For the supplied oxidation maps, first label carbon one and carbon six, then

00:26:11.612 --> 00:26:13.952
identify which end changes oxidation level.

00:26:14.992 --> 00:26:19.452
Product identity depends on the stated oxidant and conditions; a carboxylic

00:26:19.452 --> 00:26:23.932
acid name alone does not specify whether one or both terminal groups were

00:26:23.932 --> 00:26:24.412
oxidized.

00:26:24.624 --> 00:26:29.804
A pair of epimers differs in configuration at one stereogenic centre while

00:26:29.804 --> 00:26:32.724
retaining the same connectivity and the other configurations.

00:26:33.984 --> 00:26:37.024
Compare the open chain Fischer projections of three D aldohexoses.

00:26:37.804 --> 00:26:41.724
From carbon two through carbon five, glucose has hydroxyl positions right,

00:26:42.264 --> 00:26:43.144
left, right, right.

00:26:43.924 --> 00:26:47.484
Mannose has left, left, right, right, differing only at carbon two.

00:26:48.524 --> 00:26:52.544
Galactose has right, left, left, right, differing from glucose only at carbon

00:26:52.544 --> 00:26:52.944
four.

00:26:53.424 --> 00:26:57.664
Thus glucose and mannose are carbon two epimers, while glucose and galactose

00:26:57.664 --> 00:26:58.904
are carbon four epimers.

00:26:59.864 --> 00:27:04.184
Mannose and galactose differ at two centres, so they are diastereomers but not

00:27:04.184 --> 00:27:05.104
epimers of one another.

00:27:05.964 --> 00:27:09.924
All three remain D sugars because the highest numbered stereogenic centre has

00:27:09.924 --> 00:27:11.384
the same relative configuration.

00:27:12.364 --> 00:27:16.764
D does not mean positive optical rotation: configuration and the measured sign

00:27:16.764 --> 00:27:18.524
of rotation are separate descriptors.

00:27:18.524 --> 00:27:23.312
Anomers are the special cyclic comparison at the newly created anomeric

00:27:23.312 --> 00:27:23.784
centre,

00:27:23.784 --> 00:27:25.924
not a synonym for changing any Fischer position.

00:27:26.904 --> 00:27:30.524
Always identify the carbon number and projection convention before calling two

00:27:30.524 --> 00:27:31.984
sugar drawings an epimeric pair.

00:27:32.688 --> 00:27:36.188
A protein can contain covalent links of different chemical types.

00:27:37.028 --> 00:27:41.328
A peptide linkage joins a carbonyl carbon to nitrogen along a polypeptide

00:27:41.328 --> 00:27:41.748
backbone.

00:27:42.588 --> 00:27:47.428
A disulfide instead joins sulfur atoms from two cysteine side chains after

00:27:47.428 --> 00:27:49.028
oxidation of their thiol groups.

00:27:49.908 --> 00:27:54.708
In formal atom bookkeeping, two thiols become one sulfur sulfur bond with loss

00:27:54.708 --> 00:27:59.528
of two hydrogen equivalents; this is not the condensation water loss used to

00:27:59.528 --> 00:28:00.268
count peptide assembly.

00:28:01.048 --> 00:28:04.871
Consider two separate linear tripeptide chains, each with one cysteine

00:28:04.871 --> 00:28:05.308
residue.

00:28:06.028 --> 00:28:08.888
Each chain has two peptide bonds, giving four in total.

00:28:09.968 --> 00:28:13.838
Joining the cysteine side chains with one interchain disulfide adds one cross

00:28:13.838 --> 00:28:16.248
link while leaving the peptide bond count at four.

00:28:16.948 --> 00:28:21.248
The connected structure still has two polypeptide backbones, not one newly

00:28:21.248 --> 00:28:23.468
lengthened six residue peptide backbone.

00:28:24.548 --> 00:28:28.288
Reducing that disulfide can separate the chains without hydrolysing their

00:28:28.288 --> 00:28:28.948
peptide bonds.

00:28:29.688 --> 00:28:33.386
Conversely, cutting a peptide bond is a backbone change and cannot be

00:28:33.386 --> 00:28:33.868
described

00:28:33.868 --> 00:28:35.268
solely as reducing sulfur.

00:28:36.448 --> 00:28:40.648
Distinguish side chain connectivity, chain count and backbone sequence before

00:28:40.648 --> 00:28:43.568
inferring which chemical treatment changed a protein sample.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:48.800
DNA contains both covalent backbone links and noncovalent interactions between

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:49.780
complementary bases.

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:51.160
Count them separately.

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:56.940
Consider an intact linear double stranded fragment with ten base pairs, six

00:28:56.940 --> 00:28:59.980
adenine thymine pairs and four guanine cytosine pairs.

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:03.460
There are twenty nucleotides, ten in each strand.

00:29:04.220 --> 00:29:08.500
Within each linear strand, ten nucleotide units are joined by nine internal

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:13.020
phosphodiester bridges, giving eighteen internucleotide linkages in both

00:29:13.020 --> 00:29:13.500
backbones.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:18.020
A terminal phosphate does not create an extra bridge to a missing nucleotide.

00:29:18.860 --> 00:29:23.140
Using the standard paired base model, the six adenine thymine pairs contribute

00:29:23.140 --> 00:29:27.420
twelve hydrogen bonds and the four guanine cytosine pairs contribute twelve

00:29:27.420 --> 00:29:28.880
more, twenty four total.

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:33.040
These hydrogen bonds are not the covalent links that hold each individual

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:33.720
strand together.

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:38.940
Count one internucleotide phosphodiester linkage per joining phosphate bridge,

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:43.080
rather than confusing that bridge with each individual phosphorus oxygen ester

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:43.300
bond.

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:47.660
Circular closure, nicks or unpaired bases would change the assumptions.

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:52.820
Strand separation can disrupt base pairing without requiring hydrolysis of all

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:54.040
eighteen backbone linkages.

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:58.720
Always state linearity, strand length and pairing before applying a counting

00:29:58.720 --> 00:29:59.140
shortcut.

00:29:59.352 --> 00:30:03.572
Structural evidence must identify which form of a sugar is actually being

00:30:03.572 --> 00:30:03.832
tested.

00:30:04.692 --> 00:30:09.112
Unmodified glucose can react with hydroxylamine to give an oxime through its

00:30:09.112 --> 00:30:13.972
accessible carbonyl chemistry, even though cyclic hemiacetal forms predominate

00:30:13.972 --> 00:30:14.712
in water.

00:30:14.712 --> 00:30:19.492
Acetylation gives glucose pentaacetate, supporting the presence of five

00:30:19.492 --> 00:30:21.792
hydroxyl groups available for that transformation.

00:30:22.332 --> 00:30:27.052
In the usual textbook comparison, glucose pentaacetate does not react with

00:30:27.052 --> 00:30:29.992
hydroxylamine to give the analogous oxime.

00:30:29.992 --> 00:30:33.832
The protected cyclic structure lacks the freely available aldehyde behaviour

00:30:33.832 --> 00:30:36.352
assumed by a permanent open chain drawing.

00:30:37.392 --> 00:30:41.179
Acetylating the anomeric hydroxyl prevents the ordinary hemiacetal ring

00:30:41.179 --> 00:30:41.552
opening

00:30:41.552 --> 00:30:44.492
equilibrium without first changing the protected groups.

00:30:45.272 --> 00:30:49.292
This does not mean that glucose never has an open chain form, or that every

00:30:49.292 --> 00:30:50.932
acetate group is a new aldehyde.

00:30:51.412 --> 00:30:55.972
The two observations ask different questions: how many hydroxyl groups can be

00:30:55.972 --> 00:31:00.072
acetylated, and whether the tested derivative has an accessible carbonyl under

00:31:00.072 --> 00:31:01.312
the specified conditions.

00:31:02.352 --> 00:31:06.152
Hydrolysis or deprotection can change that answer, so the derivative and

00:31:06.152 --> 00:31:08.192
reaction conditions must remain explicit.

00:31:09.112 --> 00:31:12.127
Combine these observations with the other glucose evidence rather than

00:31:12.127 --> 00:31:12.472
treating

00:31:12.472 --> 00:31:14.852
one negative test as a complete structure proof.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:20.702
Complete hydrolysis can reveal peptide composition without fixing the order of

00:31:20.702 --> 00:31:21.180
residues.

00:31:22.180 --> 00:31:26.660
Suppose a supplied linear tripeptide contains two alanine residues and one

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:30.040
glycine residue, with no modified residues or cyclization.

00:31:30.740 --> 00:31:34.618
Write sequences from amino terminus to carboxyl terminus and abbreviate

00:31:34.618 --> 00:31:35.000
alanine

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:36.340
A and glycine G.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:42.787
The distinct composition compatible sequences are A A G, A G A and G A

00:31:42.787 --> 00:31:46.840
A; exchanging the two identical alanines does not make a fourth sequence.

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:52.780
Now suppose controlled partial hydrolysis identifies both A G and G A as

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:56.740
contiguous dipeptide fragments from the same pure tripeptide, with no

00:31:56.740 --> 00:32:00.120
rearrangement or transpeptidation in the stated evidence model.

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:03.980
Only A G A contains both directed adjacent pairs.

00:32:03.980 --> 00:32:09.970
A A G contains A A and A G, while G A A contains G A and A

00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:10.140
A.

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:15.005
All three candidates have two peptide bonds and the same mass computed from

00:32:15.005 --> 00:32:15.200
two

00:32:15.200 --> 00:32:17.640
free alanines plus one glycine minus two waters.

00:32:18.820 --> 00:32:22.380
Sequence evidence therefore adds information that mass and total amino acid

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:23.400
counts cannot supply.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:27.680
Real sequence determination needs appropriate controls and fragment

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:32.140
identification; this small overlap puzzle is a supporting inference exercise

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:34.340
rather than a universal sequencing method.

00:32:34.512 --> 00:32:40.152
An ionizable side chain changes which dissociation steps surround an amino

00:32:40.152 --> 00:32:41.652
acid's net neutral form.

00:32:42.772 --> 00:32:47.092
Use a supporting dominant state calculation with supplied stepwise p K a

00:32:47.092 --> 00:32:50.312
values, rather than averaging every number automatically.

00:32:51.072 --> 00:32:56.172
For an aspartic acid model, the values are two point one, three point nine and

00:32:56.172 --> 00:32:56.932
nine point eight.

00:32:57.672 --> 00:33:02.612
Increasing p H removes protons through net charge states plus one, zero, minus

00:33:02.612 --> 00:33:03.812
one and minus two.

00:33:04.412 --> 00:33:08.572
The neutral state lies between the first two steps, so its approximate

00:33:08.572 --> 00:33:12.112
isoelectric point is their average, three point zero.

00:33:12.652 --> 00:33:17.072
For a lysine model with values two point two, nine point zero and ten point

00:33:17.072 --> 00:33:22.152
five, the successive net charges are plus two, plus one, zero and minus one.

00:33:22.152 --> 00:33:26.552
Here the neutral state lies between the last two steps, giving approximate

00:33:26.552 --> 00:33:29.672
isoelectric point nine point seven five.

00:33:29.672 --> 00:33:33.332
These are supplied illustrative constants and the usual adjacent state

00:33:33.332 --> 00:33:37.132
approximation, not universal values for every solvent and temperature.

00:33:38.292 --> 00:33:42.472
At the isoelectric point the average net charge is zero; charged groups within

00:33:42.472 --> 00:33:44.412
individual zwitterions still exist.

00:33:44.412 --> 00:33:49.432
Identify every ionizable group and the charge ladder before selecting the two

00:33:49.432 --> 00:33:49.752
constants.

00:33:50.112 --> 00:33:54.742
The sugar difference between R N A and D N A affects backbone chemistry as

00:33:54.742 --> 00:33:54.992
well

00:33:54.992 --> 00:33:56.012
as molecular naming.

00:33:57.072 --> 00:34:01.472
In a supporting alkaline cleavage model, the two prime hydroxyl of an R N A

00:34:01.472 --> 00:34:05.752
ribose can lose a proton and provide a nearby oxygen nucleophile.

00:34:06.712 --> 00:34:10.392
That oxygen attacks the phosphorus of the adjacent three prime phosphate

00:34:10.392 --> 00:34:12.072
linkage within the same chain.

00:34:12.952 --> 00:34:17.732
The downstream phosphorus oxygen five prime connection breaks, leaving a two

00:34:17.732 --> 00:34:21.980
prime three prime cyclic phosphate on one fragment and a five prime hydroxyl

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:22.092
on

00:34:22.092 --> 00:34:23.892
the other after proton transfer.

00:34:24.812 --> 00:34:29.528
The initial cleavage is an intramolecular transesterification; later opening

00:34:29.528 --> 00:34:29.652
of

00:34:29.652 --> 00:34:32.672
the cyclic phosphate can give additional hydrolysis products.

00:34:33.632 --> 00:34:37.892
This is different from merely separating complementary bases or denaturing a

00:34:37.892 --> 00:34:38.152
duplex.

00:34:38.812 --> 00:34:43.812
A D N A sugar has hydrogen rather than hydroxyl at two prime, so it cannot use

00:34:43.812 --> 00:34:45.952
this same neighbouring hydroxyl attack pathway.

00:34:46.672 --> 00:34:51.452
That distinction does not mean D N A is indestructible or that every R N A

00:34:51.452 --> 00:34:53.212
linkage cleaves at an identical rate.

00:34:54.072 --> 00:34:56.312
Sequence, conformation and conditions matter.

00:34:56.952 --> 00:35:00.666
For one specified internal cleavage in a linear chain, the number of

00:35:00.666 --> 00:35:01.212
covalently

00:35:01.212 --> 00:35:05.672
connected backbone pieces increases from one to two without creating or

00:35:05.672 --> 00:35:07.032
deleting nucleotide residues.

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:11.420
Relative configuration and sequence rule configuration are different labels.

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:16.540
In the standard amino acid Fischer comparison, place the carboxyl group at the

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:21.000
top, the side chain at the bottom and the amino group on the left to describe

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:22.760
the usual L reference arrangement.

00:35:23.700 --> 00:35:26.400
L alanine has the S configuration at its alpha carbon.

00:35:27.060 --> 00:35:30.878
L cysteine instead has the R configuration there, because the sulfur

00:35:30.878 --> 00:35:31.440
containing

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:35.200
side chain changes the priority order relative to the carboxyl group.

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:39.280
This is not inversion of the L reference arrangement; it is a different

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:41.140
sequence rule ranking of attached groups.

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:45.940
Nitrogen has higher first atom priority than carbon and hydrogen, while ties

00:35:45.940 --> 00:35:48.960
between the two carbon groups are compared one layer farther out.

00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:52.620
Sulfur outranks oxygen in that later comparison for cysteine.

00:35:53.600 --> 00:35:57.500
Glycine has two identical alpha hydrogens, so it does not possess that alpha

00:35:57.500 --> 00:35:58.620
stereocenter at all.

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:03.260
The L label also does not directly specify a positive or negative optical

00:36:03.260 --> 00:36:03.740
rotation.

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:09.420
Apply the appropriate definition rather than treating L, S and levorotatory as

00:36:09.420 --> 00:36:10.360
interchangeable words.

00:36:10.584 --> 00:36:14.824
A peptide bond and a secondary structure hydrogen bond are different

00:36:14.824 --> 00:36:15.284
connections.

00:36:16.124 --> 00:36:21.064
In the ideal alpha helix pattern, a backbone carbonyl oxygen at residue i

00:36:21.064 --> 00:36:25.524
accepts a hydrogen bond from a backbone nitrogen hydrogen group four residues

00:36:25.524 --> 00:36:27.344
later, at i plus four.

00:36:27.964 --> 00:36:32.624
This is an intrachain folding interaction, not formation of another peptide

00:36:32.624 --> 00:36:34.904
bond or loss of another water molecule.

00:36:34.904 --> 00:36:40.164
For a stipulated fully helical segment of twenty residues with every required

00:36:40.164 --> 00:36:44.544
donor and acceptor available, the allowed internal pairs run from one to five

00:36:44.844 --> 00:36:46.824
through sixteen to twenty.

00:36:46.824 --> 00:36:49.824
There are sixteen such hydrogen bonds in that ideal count.

00:36:50.724 --> 00:36:54.924
The continuous unbranched peptide backbone still contains nineteen peptide

00:36:54.924 --> 00:36:56.384
bonds between twenty residues.

00:36:57.284 --> 00:37:02.184
Real chain ends, capping groups, proline residues and nonhelical regions can

00:37:02.184 --> 00:37:06.324
alter the hydrogen bonding pattern, so the simple count must not be applied

00:37:06.324 --> 00:37:08.024
indiscriminately to every protein.

00:37:09.044 --> 00:37:12.664
Beta sheet hydrogen bonds can connect different segments of one chain or

00:37:12.664 --> 00:37:15.024
different chains and need their own geometry.

00:37:15.964 --> 00:37:20.024
State the model, count the pair endpoints, and keep covalent sequence bonds

00:37:20.024 --> 00:37:21.704
separate from folding contacts.

00:37:22.944 --> 00:37:28.564
A free anomeric hemiacetal can open and close, whereas converting it to an

00:37:28.564 --> 00:37:30.624
acetal changes the reaction options.

00:37:31.544 --> 00:37:36.464
In a selected glucose comparison, replacing the anomeric hydroxyl by a methoxy

00:37:36.464 --> 00:37:39.084
group gives a methyl glucoside.

00:37:39.084 --> 00:37:43.702
The anomeric carbon is then bonded to the ring oxygen and the methoxy oxygen

00:37:43.702 --> 00:37:43.824
as

00:37:43.824 --> 00:37:44.384
an acetal.

00:37:45.244 --> 00:37:48.564
Four other free hydroxyl groups remain on the glucose framework.

00:37:48.564 --> 00:37:54.284
While this acetal stays intact, ordinary ring opening to a free aldehyde and

00:37:54.284 --> 00:37:58.904
the usual mutarotation pathway are blocked, so the alpha and beta methyl

00:37:58.904 --> 00:38:02.927
glucosides are distinct configurations rather than the ordinary aqueous

00:38:02.927 --> 00:38:03.324
glucose

00:38:03.324 --> 00:38:04.324
equilibrium pair.

00:38:05.504 --> 00:38:09.784
Suitable hydrolysis can restore glucose and methanol, after which the glucose

00:38:09.784 --> 00:38:12.284
can undergo its usual anomer equilibration.

00:38:12.834 --> 00:38:18.662
The net formation ledger is glucose plus methanol minus water, giving formula

00:38:18.662 --> 00:38:18.737
C

00:38:18.737 --> 00:38:23.784
seven H fourteen O six and rounded molar mass one hundred ninety four.

00:38:23.784 --> 00:38:28.164
This does not mean every glycosidic bond in every larger carbohydrate removes

00:38:28.164 --> 00:38:32.864
all reducing behavior; a separate free anomeric center may remain elsewhere.

00:38:34.004 --> 00:38:37.324
Check the actual anomeric connection before assigning a test result.

00:38:37.752 --> 00:38:42.858
A catalyst can become nearly saturated with substrate without being consumed

00:38:42.858 --> 00:38:42.992
by

00:38:42.992 --> 00:38:43.512
the reaction.

00:38:44.372 --> 00:38:48.812
In a supporting initial rate model for a suitable simple enzyme system, rate

00:38:48.812 --> 00:38:54.652
equals maximum rate times substrate concentration divided by K m plus

00:38:54.652 --> 00:38:55.712
substrate concentration.

00:38:56.912 --> 00:39:01.612
Hold enzyme amount and conditions fixed, and use supplied K m two millimolar

00:39:01.612 --> 00:39:04.632
with maximum rate one hundred micromolar per minute.

00:39:05.312 --> 00:39:09.272
At substrate two millimolar, rate is fifty micromolar per minute.

00:39:09.992 --> 00:39:14.872
At eight millimolar, it is eighty, so quadrupling substrate gives only a one

00:39:14.872 --> 00:39:17.112
point six fold rate increase in this comparison.

00:39:18.172 --> 00:39:22.452
At very high substrate the rate approaches the maximum rather than increasing

00:39:22.452 --> 00:39:23.172
without bound.

00:39:23.172 --> 00:39:28.772
K m is the substrate concentration giving half maximum rate in this model; it

00:39:28.772 --> 00:39:32.712
must not automatically be equated to a binding dissociation constant for every

00:39:32.712 --> 00:39:33.072
mechanism.

00:39:33.652 --> 00:39:38.452
The equation is a stipulated supporting approximation, not a claim that all

00:39:38.452 --> 00:39:40.072
enzymes follow one simple law.

00:39:40.932 --> 00:39:44.769
It does not change the reaction equilibrium constant, and an initial rate

00:39:44.769 --> 00:39:45.032
curve

00:39:45.032 --> 00:39:48.192
alone does not establish the final equilibrium composition.

00:39:48.504 --> 00:39:52.784
A finite carbohydrate chain needs an end group correction that the bulk repeat

00:39:52.784 --> 00:39:54.084
unit formula can hide.

00:39:55.124 --> 00:39:59.242
Consider a hypothetical unmodified linear oligomer containing ten glucose

00:39:59.242 --> 00:39:59.524
units

00:39:59.524 --> 00:40:02.244
joined by nine ordinary glycosidic linkages.

00:40:03.444 --> 00:40:07.584
Use rounded molar masses of one hundred eighty grams per mole for glucose and

00:40:07.584 --> 00:40:08.504
eighteen for water.

00:40:09.024 --> 00:40:13.704
Its formation balance starts with ten glucose molecules and subtracts nine

00:40:13.704 --> 00:40:18.184
water molecules, giving a molar mass of sixteen hundred thirty eight grams per

00:40:18.184 --> 00:40:18.344
mole.

00:40:19.624 --> 00:40:23.764
Complete hydrolysis consumes nine waters and produces ten glucose molecules

00:40:23.764 --> 00:40:27.844
with combined molar mass eighteen hundred grams per mole of the original

00:40:27.844 --> 00:40:28.224
oligomer.

00:40:29.484 --> 00:40:32.884
The product mass is larger because water has been incorporated from the

00:40:32.884 --> 00:40:36.224
reaction medium; glucose mass was not created from nothing.

00:40:37.244 --> 00:40:40.864
For one mole of this oligomer, the mass increase is one hundred sixty two

00:40:40.864 --> 00:40:41.164
grams.

00:40:41.864 --> 00:40:46.267
The common bulk expression based on a glucose residue mass of one hundred

00:40:46.267 --> 00:40:46.569
sixty

00:40:46.569 --> 00:40:50.204
two omits the extra terminal water contribution for a finite open chain.

00:40:51.244 --> 00:40:55.399
This example assumes no ring closure, attached protein, substitution or

00:40:55.399 --> 00:40:55.984
oxidation.

00:40:56.924 --> 00:41:01.024
Branching without cycles still leaves nine links for ten connected units, so

00:41:01.024 --> 00:41:04.964
the same overall water balance can apply even when the detailed connectivity

00:41:04.964 --> 00:41:05.384
differs.

00:41:05.664 --> 00:41:09.644
Branching and reducing end count describe different features of carbohydrate

00:41:09.644 --> 00:41:10.224
connectivity.

00:41:11.484 --> 00:41:15.824
Use a supporting abstract model of ten glucose units in one connected acyclic

00:41:15.824 --> 00:41:16.244
structure.

00:41:17.344 --> 00:41:21.724
Each glycosidic linkage consumes the anomeric centre of one child unit and

00:41:21.724 --> 00:41:24.184
joins it to an ordinary hydroxyl group of its parent.

00:41:25.584 --> 00:41:29.104
Exclude anomeric to anomeric links, ring closure and chemical caps.

00:41:29.784 --> 00:41:34.471
A connected tree of ten units has nine links, so nine anomeric centres are

00:41:34.471 --> 00:41:34.724
used

00:41:34.724 --> 00:41:36.684
and one remains free at the root.

00:41:37.684 --> 00:41:41.904
Now choose two branch points, each carrying two child arms, with every other

00:41:41.904 --> 00:41:43.524
internal unit carrying one child.

00:41:44.404 --> 00:41:47.064
This topology has three nonreducing terminal arms.

00:41:47.504 --> 00:41:51.224
It does not have three free reducing anomeric centres.

00:41:51.224 --> 00:41:54.844
Cutting one linkage without further rearrangement splits the connected tree

00:41:54.844 --> 00:41:59.224
into two components and exposes one additional free anomeric centre in this

00:41:59.224 --> 00:41:59.404
model.

00:42:00.624 --> 00:42:04.264
These are molecular inventory counts, not a prediction of the intensity of a

00:42:04.264 --> 00:42:06.324
reducing sugar test for a large polymer.

00:42:07.444 --> 00:42:11.724
Real glycogen also has a protein associated reducing end, which this uncapped

00:42:11.724 --> 00:42:13.584
exercise deliberately excludes.

00:42:14.544 --> 00:42:18.384
Always define which carbon participates in each linkage before turning a

00:42:18.384 --> 00:42:19.864
branched drawing into an end count.

00:42:20.112 --> 00:42:24.647
The charge of a short peptide cannot be found by adding the charges of its

00:42:24.647 --> 00:42:24.892
free

00:42:24.892 --> 00:42:27.012
amino acid building blocks unchanged.

00:42:28.172 --> 00:42:32.572
Peptide formation converts internal backbone amino and carboxyl groups into

00:42:32.572 --> 00:42:33.552
amide linkages.

00:42:34.872 --> 00:42:39.552
Consider the linear sequence lysine glycine lysine with unmodified free amino

00:42:39.552 --> 00:42:40.692
and carboxyl termini.

00:42:41.412 --> 00:42:46.592
In a stipulated dominant protonation state near neutral pH, assign plus one to

00:42:46.592 --> 00:42:51.932
the amino terminus, minus one to the carboxyl terminus and plus one to each of

00:42:51.932 --> 00:42:54.072
the two lysine side chain amino groups.

00:42:54.992 --> 00:42:57.672
Glycine has no ionizable side chain in this model.

00:42:58.392 --> 00:43:00.392
The net charge is therefore plus two.

00:43:01.112 --> 00:43:02.992
There are two backbone peptide linkages.

00:43:03.712 --> 00:43:07.612
These amides do not act as additional simple amine sites under the stated

00:43:07.612 --> 00:43:08.172
conditions.

00:43:08.872 --> 00:43:12.712
If only the amino terminus is acetylated and all other stated charge

00:43:12.712 --> 00:43:17.152
assignments remain unchanged, removal of that terminal positive charge changes

00:43:17.152 --> 00:43:19.432
the net charge in this example to plus one.

00:43:20.172 --> 00:43:24.512
This is formal dominant state bookkeeping, not an exact fractional population

00:43:24.512 --> 00:43:25.872
calculation for every pH.

00:43:26.712 --> 00:43:29.612
Real site acid constants depend on the molecular environment.

00:43:30.592 --> 00:43:34.312
State terminal modifications, side chain identities and protonation conditions

00:43:34.312 --> 00:43:37.632
before comparing peptide charge or migration direction.

00:43:37.824 --> 00:43:41.444
Knowing the number of cysteine residues does not uniquely specify which

00:43:41.444 --> 00:43:43.264
disulfide bonds a protein contains.

00:43:44.144 --> 00:43:48.164
In a supporting connectivity model, label four cysteine side chains by their

00:43:48.164 --> 00:43:53.104
positions one, two, three and four in a single fixed polypeptide sequence.

00:43:54.084 --> 00:43:58.524
Assume every thiol forms exactly one intramolecular disulfide and no other

00:43:58.524 --> 00:43:59.304
reaction occurs.

00:44:00.224 --> 00:44:03.224
Site one can pair with site two, site three or site four.

00:44:03.744 --> 00:44:07.244
Once that partner is selected, the remaining two sites must pair with each

00:44:07.244 --> 00:44:07.584
other.

00:44:08.124 --> 00:44:12.064
The three possibilities are therefore one with two and three with four; one

00:44:12.064 --> 00:44:15.564
with three and two with four; or one with four and two with three.

00:44:16.404 --> 00:44:19.764
Exchanging the order in which the same two links are listed does not make a

00:44:19.764 --> 00:44:20.304
fourth structure.

00:44:20.964 --> 00:44:24.924
Each pattern has two sulfur sulfur links and the same backbone peptide bond

00:44:24.924 --> 00:44:25.444
inventory.

00:44:26.124 --> 00:44:30.644
Formal oxidation of four thiols removes four hydrogen equivalents; it is not

00:44:30.644 --> 00:44:32.484
the loss of two condensation waters.

00:44:33.444 --> 00:44:37.444
Complete reduction restores four thiols without requiring peptide hydrolysis.

00:44:38.144 --> 00:44:42.304
The three combinatorial patterns are possible pairings, not a claim that they

00:44:42.304 --> 00:44:44.084
are equally stable or all accessible.

00:44:44.984 --> 00:44:48.264
Folding geometry and chemical evidence are needed to identify the actual

00:44:48.264 --> 00:44:49.124
disulfide arrangement.
